      \begin{22enumerate}

      \item The Sun's favorite logical operator $\star$ has the following property on the logical propositions $x$ and $y$
      \[x\star y\equiv (\text{NOT $x$})\text{ OR } (\text{NOT $y$})\]

      where $\equiv$ is the notation for equivalence, meaning the left and the right expressions are equivalent. Create a truth table for each of the following expressions, and state which single logical operator on $x$ and/or $y$ the expression is equivalent to.
      
      \begin{enumerate}[i.]
      \item $x\star x$
      \item $(x\star y)\star (x\star y)$
      \item $(x\star x)\star (y\star y)$
      \item $(x\star (x\star y))\star (y\star(y\star x))$
      \end{enumerate}
      
      \item The Sun has been awake for 4.603 billion years wondering the following:
      Can we express the OR operation using only
      IMPLIES operations? Justify your answer and help the poor Sun sleep.
\end{22enumerate}
}
