      For the first time in millions of years, the \personA, \personB, \personC\;and the \personD\;are aligned. To celebrate this `syzygy', they decide to play a dangerous game of asteroid gravitational deflection.
      The game has the following rules:
      
      \begin{enumerate} [I.] \itemsep0pt
      \item \personB\;loses if and only if the \personD\;gets hit with an asteroid.
      
      \item If the \personD\;orbits faster to get away, then \personB\;deflects an asteroid towards \personC.
      
      \item If \personB\;deflects an asteroid towards \personC, then the \personA\;yells at \personB.
      
      \item The \personD\;orbits faster to get away if and only if it does not get hit by another player with an asteroid.
      
      \item If the \personA\;yells at \personB, then the \personD\;does not get hit with an asteroid.
      
      \item Every player must either win or lose.
      
      \item If \personB\;does not deflect an asteroid towards \personC, then \personC\;wins.
      
      \item Only one player can win.
      \end{enumerate}

      Using the eight rules, prove the following statements. Recall that if and only if statements require proof in both directions of implication.
     
	  \begin{22enumerate} \itemsep0pt
	  \item \personB\;wins if and only if it deflects an asteroid towards \personC.
	  \item The \personD\;gets  hit with an asteroid if and only if the \personA\;does not yell at \personB.
	  \item The statement ``The \personD\;does not win'' is valid (under the definition given in lecture).
	  \end{22enumerate}
}
