    As a reminder, Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF) is a particular way of writing logical expressions. We define it as follows:
      \begin{itemize}
      \item A literal is a variable or its negation: $x_1$ and NOT($x_1$) are 
      examples of literals.

      \item A clause is any disjunction (OR) of any number of literals: $(x_1$ OR $x_2)$ or a lone literal are examples of clauses.

      \item An expression is in conjunctive normal form (CNF) if it is the conjunction (AND) of any number of clauses. The following is an example of a CNF expression.
      \[(x_1 \text{ OR }x_2) \text{ AND }(x_3\text{ OR } \text{NOT} (x_1))\text{ AND }x_4\]
      \end{itemize}    

      For each of the following expressions, convert it to CNF. Using 
      truth tables or logical identities,  prove your converted expression is logically equivalent to the given one.   
      
     
      \begin{22enumerate} 

      \item $( p$ AND NOT($q))$ OR NOT($p$) 

      \item $(p$ AND NOT($q$)) OR (NOT($p$) AND q)

      \item NOT($p$ AND ($q$ OR $r$))

 
      \item $(p$ IMPLIES $q)$ IMPLIES $r$ 
      \end{22enumerate}
}
